  "assumptions": [
    "Illustration: a made-up two-setting loss bowl with simulated batch noise, not a real model trained on real data.",
    "Loss = (3x² + z²)/2, drawn at height 0.5 × loss over x, z in [-2, 2]. It is 3 times steeper in x, so steps overshoot in x above learning rate 1/3 and grow (diverge) above 2/3.",
    "Each step: settings −= learning rate × (true slope + noise). Noise sd = 2·√((64−B)/(63·B)), i.e. sampling B of 64 points; exactly zero at B = 64. Noise continues along a fixed-seed sequence across new runs.",
    "Orange arrow = the next mini-batch step (learning rate × noisy slope, pointing the way the ball moves). Grey arrow = learning rate × true slope. Both are drawn 3 × their true length.",
    "A run ends at 40 steps (finished) or when the loss passes 8 or the ball leaves the drawn bowl (diverged). The ball is then drawn at the bowl's edge, and the loss readout keeps the true value.",
    "Each host step is one update, so at speed 1 a 40-step run takes about 1.3 s. Use a slower speed or Step once to follow it.",
    "The trail marks where the ball landed after each of up to 40 steps. The ghost keeps the last run's path."
  ]
}